Object.assign(ARTICLES, {"wwf-wrestlefest-preview": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · WrestleFest '91 · Busch Stadium, July 14", "title": "Hogan vs. Slaughter", "dek": "Twelve matches, one baseball stadium and a main event everybody thinks they already know the finish to. The interesting part of WrestleFest is everything around it.", "body": [["p", "WrestleFest '91 is Sunday night at Busch Stadium in St. Louis, live on pay-per-view, and the main event is the one the WWF has been selling since the spring: Hulk Hogan defending the WWF Title against Sgt. Slaughter, with General Adnan in Slaughter's corner. Hogan picked Hacksaw Jim Duggan as special enforcer on the Superstars taping that airs Saturday morning, which is the WWF's way of saying the Adnan interference everyone expects isn't going to work."], ["p", "Nobody in the business thinks Slaughter is winning. The Iraqi sympathizer gimmick drew its money earlier in the year, and the house-show tags with Warlord in Canada (Hogan & Duggan over Slaughter & Warlord in Calgary, 3/4* in 21:52) haven't exactly reignited it. The question is what happens after the bell, and the answer is probably standing at ringside in Saskatoon staring at the belt. More on Sid Justice elsewhere."], ["h", "The undercard"], ["p", "The rest of the card is deeper than people realize. The Nasty Boys defend the tag titles against the Rockers, a rematch of the MSG DQ. Warrior and the Undertaker go again after three straight Warrior wins. Mr. Perfect defends the Intercontinental Title against Greg Valentine, who replaced Davey Boy Smith and is coming off a neck injury, so that match is expected to be kept around 12 minutes. The Mountie faces Tito Santana with Big Boss Man at ringside to keep Jimmy Hart honest. Virgil gets Ted DiBiase. Jake Roberts gets Typhoon, who replaced the injured Earthquake. LOD face Power & Glory. Tenryu and Kitao from SWS face Demolition. Warlord faces Snuka. Haku and the Barbarian get the Orient Express. The Beverly Brothers open against High Energy."], ["p", "There's also a Steamboat injury update and a Barbershop segment with Randy Savage and Miss Elizabeth, which is the clearest sign yet that Savage is being kept around for something bigger later in the summer."], ["h", "The competition"], ["p", "It's the same night as WCW's Great American Bash in Baltimore. The WWF will win on attendance by tens of thousands. Whether it wins on the Monday-morning conversation depends on what Sid does."]], "facts": [["Event", "WrestleFest '91 (PPV)"], ["Venue", "Busch Stadium, St. Louis"], ["Main event", "Hogan vs. Slaughter, WWF Title"], ["Enforcer", "Jim Duggan"], ["Matches", "12, plus two segments"], ["Same night", "WCW Great American Bash"]], "factTitle": "The Card"}, "wwf-sid-is-watching": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · The Next Main Event", "title": "Sid Is Watching", "dek": "Sid Justice saved Hulk Hogan on TV, didn't get picked as his enforcer, and has spent the week following him around Canada. You can see where this is going.", "body": [["p", "If you want to know what the WWF's next main event is, watch where Sid Justice is standing."], ["p", "At the Superstars taping in Vancouver on July 7, which airs Saturday, Sid made the save when Sgt. Slaughter and General Adnan jumped Hogan after a TV squash. Hogan raised his hand. Sid pulled his arm away and stared at the belt on Hogan's shoulder. Then, in the Mean Gene interview, Hogan thanked Sid and named Jim Duggan as his WrestleFest enforcer instead. Backstage, Harvey Wippleman asked the obvious question (\"After what Sid did for you?\") and Sid said Hogan owes him, and he always collects."], ["h", "On the road"], ["p", "The house shows have kept it going. In Calgary on July 8, Wippleman jawed at Hogan from the floor after Hogan pinned Warlord in the main-event tag. In Edmonton, Sid squashed Brutus Beefcake with the powerbomb in a match that rated minus ***1/4, which says more about Beefcake than Sid. In Saskatoon on July 10, Sid powerbombed Hercules (**), then sat at ringside for the whole Hogan vs. Warlord main event and stared at the belt when it was over."], ["p", "That main event rated DUD at 12 minutes. Warlord isn't a singles main-eventer and the office has now seen that twice. Sid, standing at ringside doing nothing, was the most over thing in the match."], ["h", "The read"], ["p", "Hogan vs. Slaughter is the past. Hogan vs. Sid is the future, and it's being built patiently, which is unusual for the WWF in 1991. Sid is 6-foot-9, he's got the look, and the powerbomb gets a reaction everywhere. The question is whether he can carry his end of a 15-minute main event against Hogan, because nothing he's done so far has gone longer than about seven. The smart money says something happens in St. Louis after the bell. SummerSlam is August 25 at Madison Square Garden."]], "facts": [["7/7 Vancouver", "Saves Hogan, stares at belt"], ["7/7 taping", "Hogan picks Duggan instead"], ["7/8 Calgary", "Wippleman jaws at Hogan"], ["7/10 Saskatoon", "At ringside for Hogan vs. Warlord"], ["Manager", "Harvey Wippleman"]], "factTitle": "Sid's Week"}, "wwf-edmonton-injuries": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · Northlands Coliseum, Edmonton · July 9", "title": "The Edmonton Injuries", "dek": "The WWF drew 10,050 in Edmonton and lost three wrestlers in one night, one of them a WrestleFest headliner.", "body": [["p", "Edmonton on July 9 drew 10,050 for $201,000, the third-best house of the month, and it may be remembered as the night the WrestleFest card had to be rebuilt."], ["p", "Earthquake hurt his neck in the main event, a tag with the Undertaker against Warrior and Jake Roberts that ended when Warrior pinned him with the splash. He's out about five dates, which takes him through WrestleFest. Greg Valentine hurt his neck in the Virgil & Valentine vs. Money Inc. tag (**3/4, Virgil pinned IRS with a roll-up), and he's out about three dates. Bushwhacker Butch hurt his elbow in a 17-minute Bushwhackers win over the Orient Express that was supposed to go eight and rated **3/4 anyway."], ["h", "The fallout"], ["p", "Earthquake was booked against Jake Roberts at WrestleFest. Typhoon, his Natural Disasters partner, replaces him, and the TV is being patched with inserts recorded after the injury to explain it. Valentine was already a replacement, for Davey Boy Smith against Mr. Perfect for the Intercontinental Title, and he's staying in the match. He's sitting out the house shows until Sunday, and the match is being cut to around 12 minutes."], ["h", "The rest of the show"], ["p", "The card itself rated *1/2. The co-best matches were Tenryu and Kitao over Power & Glory (Tenryu powerbombed Roma) and the Rockers over the Beverly Brothers, both at ***1/4. Perfect beat Owen Hart in a non-title match with Heenan's help (**3/4). The worst was Sid vs. Beefcake at minus ***1/4. That's the second time Beefcake has gotten that exact rating this month."], ["p", "The real story, though, was after the main event. Undertaker and Paul Bearer jumped the Warrior, and Jake Roberts waited before he went in to help. More on that elsewhere."]], "facts": [["Crowd", "10,050 · $201,000"], ["Card", "*1/2"], ["Earthquake", "Neck, ~5 cards, misses WrestleFest"], ["Valentine", "Neck, ~3 cards, back for WrestleFest"], ["Butch", "Elbow, ~1 card"], ["Replacement", "Typhoon vs. Jake at WrestleFest"]], "factTitle": "Injury Report"}, "wwf-jake-hesitates": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · Jake Roberts", "title": "Jake Hesitates", "dek": "Jake Roberts didn't show up at the Garden, beat Earthquake twice, and then, in Edmonton, stood on the apron a little too long while the Warrior was getting beaten up. It wasn't an accident.", "body": [["p", "Jake Roberts' July started with a no-show. He was booked against Earthquake at Madison Square Garden on July 1 and didn't appear. Jim Duggan took the match and beat Earthquake (1/4*), and nobody has explained why Jake wasn't there."], ["p", "He came back and beat Earthquake with the DDT in Des Moines on July 5 (*1/4) and again in Anaheim on July 6 (*), where Damien came out of the bag and scattered the Hart camp. The feud wasn't drawing great ratings, but it was getting a reaction."], ["h", "Edmonton"], ["p", "Then came the July 9 main event in Edmonton. Jake teamed with the Ultimate Warrior against the Undertaker and Earthquake. Warrior pinned Earthquake with the splash. After the match, Undertaker and Paul Bearer jumped Warrior, and Jake hesitated. He stood there for a beat before going in. In a house show without cameras, very few people would notice that unless they were meant to."], ["p", "That's a seed. Jake has been the darkest babyface in the company for years, and Warrior and the Undertaker are in a feud built around darkness and caskets. A Jake turn on the Warrior is the kind of story that could carry the fall. If it's coming, it's being planted quietly, which is the right way to do it."], ["h", "WrestleFest"], ["p", "In St. Louis, Jake gets Typhoon, who replaces his injured partner. Jake promised on TV that it doesn't matter which one of them shows up. If he's involved in anything after the Warrior vs. Undertaker match, pay attention to how quickly he moves."]], "facts": [["7/1 MSG", "No-show vs. Earthquake"], ["7/5 Des Moines", "d. Earthquake (DDT)"], ["7/6 Anaheim", "d. Earthquake (DDT), Damien"], ["7/9 Edmonton", "w/ Warrior; hesitated on the save"], ["WrestleFest", "vs. Typhoon"]], "factTitle": "Jake's July"}, "wwf-nastys-survive": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · Saskatchewan Place, Saskatoon · July 10", "title": "Nastys Survive", "dek": "The Nasty Boys kept the tag titles on a count-out against the Legion of Doom, and the man in LOD's corner wasn't supposed to be there.", "body": [["p", "Saskatoon on July 10 drew 6,500 at a $17 average for $110,500, the softest Canadian house of the swing. The semi-main was the Nasty Boys defending the WWF Tag Team Titles against the Legion of Doom. Jerry Sags beat Hawk by count-out at 16:39, so the Nastys kept the belts, and then LOD hit the Doomsday Device on Brian Knobbs after the bell. It rated *1/2."], ["p", "That's the second straight tag-title defense the Nastys have survived without winning cleanly. At the Garden on July 1 they kept the belts when Sags beat Shawn Michaels by DQ with Jimmy Hart involved (**1/4). They're champions who keep finding the exit."], ["h", "About Ellering"], ["p", "The detail people noticed was Paul Ellering, LOD's longtime manager, at ringside and getting involved against Sags. The problem is that Ellering isn't on the WWF's list of managers, and he's currently in Memphis managing Andre the Giant in the USWA. Either the WWF is planning something with him that nobody has announced, or someone forgot who works where. Either way, expect it to be either explained or quietly dropped."], ["h", "Where the belts go"], ["p", "The Nastys defend against the Rockers again at WrestleFest. LOD are booked against Power & Glory on the same show, which reads like a contenders' match. If the titles change hands in St. Louis, LOD look like the next challengers. Money Inc. are lurking too."], ["h", "Also"], ["p", "Big Bubba Rogers is no longer with the WWF. He was originally on the Saskatoon card and was pulled."]], "facts": [["Match", "Nasty Boys (c) vs. LOD"], ["Result", "Sags beats Hawk by count-out"], ["Post-match", "Doomsday Device on Knobbs"], ["Rating", "*1/2"], ["Crowd", "6,500 · $110,500"], ["Next", "vs. Rockers, WrestleFest"]], "factTitle": "The Title Match"}, "wwf-owen-steals-calgary": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-09", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Calgary, Olympic Saddledome · July 8", "title": "Owen Steals the Show", "dek": "13,000 paid $260,000 in Calgary for the best WWF card of the month. Owen Hart had the match of the night at home, and every angle that matters for St. Louis moved forward.", "body": [["p", "WWF ran the Olympic Saddledome in Calgary on Monday, July 8, the night after the Superstars tapings in Vancouver, and drew 13,000 paying $260,000 at a $20 average ticket. That's the second-biggest gate of the month behind MSG. The card came in at **, the best-rated show of the July loop so far."], ["h", "Who wasn't there"], ["p", "Calgary is Davey Boy Smith's home market, and the British Bulldog wasn't on the card because he's finishing out a full All Japan tour that runs through the Budokan show on July 27. That has not gone over well with the WWF office. He also misses WrestleFest, and Greg Valentine was moved into his Intercontinental Title shot with Mr. Perfect. Nobody is talking about it publicly, and the absence wasn't acknowledged to the crowd."], ["h", "The good stuff"], ["p", "Owen Hart vs. Rick Martel was the match of the night at ***1/2. Owen won with the missile dropkick at 18:10, and the building was with him for every near fall. SATO, one of the SWS wrestlers working the loop, pinned Shane Douglas with a layback suplex at 10:21 for ***. Legion of Doom beat Money Inc. at 17:09 (***) when Animal pinned IRS with a scoop powerslam after Virgil came down and cut IRS off at ringside."], ["h", "The angles"], ["p", "Big Boss Man beat Earthquake by DQ when Jimmy Hart got involved, and then the Mountie ran in and handcuffed Boss Man to the ropes. Expect a Jailhouse Match before long. Mr. Perfect kept the IC Title over Tito Santana with Bobby Heenan's help. In the main event, Hulk Hogan and Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter and Warlord, and Harvey Wippleman stood on the floor jawing at Hogan afterward. Wippleman's other client is Sid Justice, who made the save for Hogan in Vancouver the night before and then watched Hogan pick Duggan as his WrestleFest enforcer."], ["h", "The bad stuff"], ["p", "Perfect vs. Tito went 24:16 for **1/4, way too long for what it was. The main event went 21:52 for 3/4*. Warlord matches over 20 minutes keep rating poorly. The Rockers beat the Orient Express at **1/4, and the Undertaker beat Jimmy Snuka with the Tombstone in a short 7:34. The Ultimate Warrior was not on the card."], ["p", "Bottom line: the strongest card of the loop, carried by Owen, SATO and LOD, with the Mountie and Boss Man, Virgil and Money Inc., and Wippleman and Hogan all moving toward St. Louis."]], "facts": [["Attendance", "13,000"], ["Gate", "$260,000 ($20 avg)"], ["Card", "**"], ["Match of the night", "Owen Hart vs. Rick Martel, ***1/2"], ["Angles", "Mountie cuffs Boss Man · Virgil cuts off IRS · Heenan costs Tito · Wippleman jaws at Hogan"]], "factTitle": "Calgary"}, "wwf-garden-gold": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-09", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · New York, Des Moines, Anaheim · July 1–6", "title": "Garden Gold, Road Rust", "dek": "The Garden drew $543,200 for a *1/2 card. Then the Warrior and Undertaker loop went to Des Moines and Anaheim, and the shows got worse.", "body": [["p", "WWF ran Madison Square Garden on July 1 and drew 15,520 paying $543,200 at a $35 average ticket. That one show is most of the money the company has recorded for July. The card rated *1/2, and most of the blame goes to a casket match that the official timing has going past the 50-minute mark."], ["h", "The Garden"], ["p", "Ricky Steamboat vs. Hercules was the match of the night at ****1/4, and nothing else was close. The surprise was Warlord vs. Greg Valentine at ***1/4. The Nasty Boys kept the tag titles against the Rockers by DQ after Jimmy Hart got involved. Jake Roberts no-showed his match with Earthquake, and Jim Duggan went 18:45 with Earthquake in his place, which was about ten minutes longer than that pairing can carry."], ["p", "Warrior beat the Undertaker in the casket match, rated *. The crowd stayed with it, and the Garden came unglued when Warrior shut the lid, but no casket match on a house show should go that long."], ["h", "Des Moines"], ["p", "Four nights later, 4,500 paid $90,000 in Des Moines for a 1/4* card. Steamboat tore his right ACL against Skinner and was pinned at 3:47. Mr. Perfect over Davey Boy Smith (***1/2) was the only thing that worked. Power & Glory beat the Bushwhackers in 36 seconds, Warlord and Virgil went 24:49 for -*1/2, and Warrior beat Taker by DQ at -1/4* when Paul Bearer used the urn."], ["h", "Anaheim"], ["p", "The loop closed in Anaheim with 5,279 paying $105,580. The Mountie made Tito Santana pass out in the Carotid Control Technique at 19:56, the best match of the week at ****. Jake beat Earthquake again and sent the Hart camp running with Damien. Crush over Brutus Beefcake by countout rated -***1/4, one of the worst numbers on any card this year. Warrior bagged the Undertaker in the main event."], ["p", "Three nights, three Warrior wins, and the ratings went *, -1/4* and unrated. The program is drawing. The matches aren't delivering."]], "facts": [["MSG 7/1", "15,520 · $543,200 · *1/2"], ["Des Moines 7/5", "4,500 · $90,000 · 1/4*"], ["Anaheim 7/6", "5,279 · $105,580 · 1/2*"], ["Best match", "Steamboat vs. Hercules, ****1/4"], ["Worst match", "Crush vs. Beefcake, -***1/4"]], "factTitle": "The Week"}, "wwf-cuffed": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · Big Boss Man vs. The Mountie", "title": "Cuffed", "dek": "The Mountie handcuffed Big Boss Man to the ropes in Calgary. Boss Man is going to be at ringside at WrestleFest, and the two lawmen are on a collision course.", "body": [["p", "The best new feud in the WWF is two cops who don't like each other."], ["p", "It started in Anaheim on July 6, when Jimmy Hart interfered against Big Boss Man in his 23:52 win over Dino Bravo (**). The same night, the Mountie, also with Hart, beat Tito Santana with the Carotid Control Technique in what was the best match of the WWF's first week, ****. In Calgary on July 8, Boss Man beat Earthquake by DQ after Hart got involved again, and then the Mountie came in and handcuffed Boss Man to the ropes. In Saskatoon, Boss Man beat Bravo again (1/4*) with Hart interfering again."], ["h", "The setup"], ["p", "On the Superstars taping that airs Saturday, Boss Man says Hart cost him in Anaheim and he'll be at ringside for Mountie vs. Tito at WrestleFest to keep Hart honest. The Mountie answered that Boss Man is no lawman, and he always gets his man."], ["p", "That's a well-built program. Tito vs. the Mountie has already proven it can go ****. Boss Man at ringside gives the match a second story. And the payoff is obvious: Boss Man vs. the Mountie, probably with some kind of jail stipulation, sometime in August."], ["h", "The concern"], ["p", "The Boss Man matches with Bravo have been long and bad, ** at 23:52 and 1/4* in Saskatoon. Keep the Boss Man's matches short until the Mountie match, and let the angle do the work."]], "facts": [["7/6 Anaheim", "Mountie d. Tito, ****"], ["7/8 Calgary", "Mountie cuffs Boss Man to the ropes"], ["Manager", "Jimmy Hart (Mountie)"], ["WrestleFest", "Mountie vs. Tito, Boss Man at ringside"]], "factTitle": "The Feud"}, "wwf-virgil-strikes-back": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · Virgil vs. Ted DiBiase", "title": "Virgil Strikes Back", "dek": "Ted DiBiase's former bodyguard has spent July getting closer and closer to Money Inc. At WrestleFest he gets the Million Dollar Man himself.", "body": [["p", "Virgil's summer has been a slow climb toward Ted DiBiase. In Anaheim on July 6, he and Shane Douglas lost to Money Inc. when DiBiase beat Douglas by DQ in 2:13, with the British Bulldog getting involved against DiBiase. In Calgary on July 8, Virgil cut IRS off at ringside during the LOD vs. Money Inc. tag, which LOD won when Animal pinned IRS. In Edmonton on July 9, Virgil pinned IRS himself with a roll-up in a tag with Greg Valentine (**3/4)."], ["p", "That last one matters. A pin over IRS is a pin over half of Money Inc., and it earns Virgil the singles match he's been after."], ["h", "WrestleFest"], ["p", "In St. Louis, Virgil faces DiBiase with IRS and Jimmy Hart in DiBiase's corner. On the Superstars taping, Virgil says he's done carrying DiBiase's bags. DiBiase says everybody has a price."], ["p", "With two men in DiBiase's corner, the likeliest result is that Virgil gets screwed and the chase goes on. That's fine. The Million Dollar Championship is the right prop for a long chase, and Virgil has been one of the more reliable workers on the house shows. His Des Moines match with Warlord (24:49, minus *1/2) was a disaster, but nobody blames him for that one."]], "facts": [["7/6 Anaheim", "Lost w/ Douglas to Money Inc. (DQ)"], ["7/8 Calgary", "Cuts off IRS at ringside"], ["7/9 Edmonton", "Pins IRS (roll-up)"], ["WrestleFest", "vs. Ted DiBiase"], ["Champion", "DiBiase, Million Dollar Championship"]], "factTitle": "The Chase"}, "wwf-sws-experiment": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · SWS Talent", "title": "The SWS Experiment", "dek": "Genichiro Tenryu, Koji Kitao and SATO are working the WWF loop as part of the SWS relationship. Two out of three shows, they've been among the best things on the card.", "body": [["p", "The WWF's working arrangement with Japan's SWS has three of its wrestlers on the July tour: Genichiro Tenryu, former sumo grand champion Koji Kitao, and the junior SATO."], ["p", "SATO opened the Calgary show on July 8 by beating Shane Douglas with a layback suplex, ***, one of the three best matches on the best card of the month. Tenryu and Kitao beat Power & Glory in Edmonton on July 9 when Tenryu powerbombed Paul Roma, ***1/4, co-match of the night. For a company that mostly does short matches with big men, those were a change of pace, and the crowds responded."], ["h", "Saskatoon"], ["p", "Then there was Saskatoon on July 10. SATO was supposed to beat Skinner in the opener, and instead Skinner won by DQ in 42 seconds. That rated *. Whatever happened, it wasn't the plan."], ["h", "WrestleFest"], ["p", "Tenryu and Kitao get Demolition (Ax and Smash) at WrestleFest. Superstars is airing a profile on them Saturday, \"From the Land of the Rising Sun,\" with SWS footage and Kitao's sumo history. It's a showcase match, and on paper it's the right one. Demolition are big enough to make Kitao look like a monster and experienced enough to work with Tenryu."], ["p", "The bigger question is what the relationship is for. If it's just a few weeks of novelty matches, fine. If the WWF wants Tenryu as a regular part of the show, he's good enough to be in the top half of the card."]], "facts": [["SATO 7/8", "d. Shane Douglas, ***"], ["Tenryu & Kitao 7/9", "d. Power & Glory, ***1/4"], ["SATO 7/10", "Lost by DQ to Skinner, 0:42"], ["WrestleFest", "Tenryu & Kitao vs. Demolition"]], "factTitle": "SWS in the WWF"}, "wwf-bulldog-two-masters": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "region": "WWF Report", "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-11", "kicker": "WWF · Backstage", "title": "The Bulldog's Two Masters", "dek": "Davey Boy Smith is finishing All Japan's summer tour instead of working the WWF's Canadian swing and WrestleFest. The office isn't thrilled about it.", "body": [["p", "Davey Boy Smith's schedule this month is a mess, and the WWF isn't happy about it."], ["p", "He's committed to All Japan's Summer Action Series, teaming with the Dynamite Kid as the British Bulldogs, and he's finishing the whole tour through the Budokan finale on July 27. That means he isn't expected back for the WWF until about July 29. He missed the Calgary show on July 8, a card that had been built around him and Owen Hart in their home market, and he's off WrestleFest. Greg Valentine replaced him against Mr. Perfect for the Intercontinental Title."], ["p", "Making it stranger, Davey did appear for the WWF in Des Moines on July 5, where he lost an excellent IC title match to Perfect (***1/2) and got mocked by Heenan afterward, and in Anaheim on July 6, where he interfered against DiBiase. Both of those dates overlap his All Japan schedule. It's the kind of thing that happens when two promotions think they have the same guy."], ["h", "The friction"], ["p", "The word is that there is real friction between Davey and the WWF office over the Japan commitment. Losing a top babyface for most of July, during a stretch where Steamboat is already hurt and three more guys went down in Edmonton, isn't a small thing. On TV, none of it is being mentioned. Valentine simply challenged Perfect on Superstars, and the WWF is acting as though Davey was never booked."], ["h", "When he's back"], ["p", "Davey vs. Perfect was the best thing the Intercontinental Title has done all month. If the relationship is patched up by August, that's an obvious rematch. If it isn't, the WWF may decide it doesn't need to share him."]], "facts": [["Working", "AJPW Summer Action Series"], ["Back for WWF", "About 7/29"], ["Missed", "Calgary 7/8, WrestleFest 7/14"], ["Replaced by", "Greg Valentine vs. Perfect"], ["Last WWF match", "Lost to Perfect 7/5, ***1/2"]], "factTitle": "The Situation"}, "wwf-steamboat-acl": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-09", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Injury Report", "title": "Steamboat Down", "dek": "Ricky Steamboat tore his right ACL in Des Moines on July 5. Nobody agrees on how long he'll be out, and the WWF just lost the best pure wrestler it had.", "body": [["p", "Ricky Steamboat tore the anterior cruciate ligament in his right knee in a 3:47 loss to Skinner at the WWF house show in Des Moines on July 5. How long he's out depends on who you ask. One estimate going around the office is about a dozen more dates. The other is closer to ten months, which would put any return around next spring. Four nights earlier he'd had the best match at Madison Square Garden, ****1/4 with Hercules."], ["p", "The show itself was the Ultimate Warrior vs. The Undertaker program's middle stop, between the Coffin Match at Madison Square Garden on July 1 and the Bodybag Match in Anaheim the next night. Nobody was talking about any of that by the end of the week."], ["h", "What they lose"], ["p", "Steamboat is the best working babyface in the company and maybe in the country. The WWF runs on Hogan and Warrior on top, with workers like Steamboat underneath keeping the matches good. Without him, the card gets thinner exactly where it can least afford it, especially with a heavy Canadian swing on the schedule and WrestleFest at Busch Stadium coming up."], ["p", "There's also the Jake Roberts situation. Roberts no-showed his MSG match with Earthquake on July 1. Losing Steamboat to injury and having Roberts' status in question in the same week isn't great for the depth of the babyface roster."], ["h", "Spring"], ["p", "The good news, if there is any, is that a long injury gives you a natural return date. A Steamboat comeback in the spring of 1992 is a ready-made story, and the WWF has always been good at bringing people back to a big pop. Somebody should start planning it now."]], "facts": [["Injury", "Torn right ACL"], ["When", "Des Moines, July 5, vs. Skinner"], ["Out", "12 cards to 10 months (TBD)"], ["Return", "To be ruled"], ["Also", "Jake Roberts no-show, MSG 7/1"]], "factTitle": "Injury Report"}, "wwf-observer-notes-canada": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-12", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Backstage Notes", "title": "Backstage Notes: The Canadian Swing", "dek": "Davey Boy Smith misses his home crowd for Japan, Jake hesitates, Snuka misses the drive, and Jannetty's deal is coming due.", "factTitle": "Filed from", "facts": [["Shows", "Calgary 7/8 · Edmonton 7/9 · Regina 7/11"], ["Best gate", "Calgary, 13,000 · $260,000"]], "body": [["h", "Calgary, AB"], ["p", "Davey Boy Smith missed Calgary, in his home territory, because he's finishing All Japan's Summer Action Series through 7/27. The WWF office isn't happy about losing him for most of July."], ["h", "Edmonton, AB"], ["p", "Jake Roberts hesitated before helping the Ultimate Warrior after the Undertaker and Paul Bearer jumped him following the main event."], ["h", "Regina, SK"], ["p", "Jimmy Snuka no-showed Regina. The story backstage is that he missed the drive from Saskatoon, either because he overslept or because of car trouble outside Davidson. He showed up in Winnipeg the next night."], ["h", "Regina, SK"], ["p", "Marty Jannetty's contract is coming due."]]}, "world-who-gets-paid": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-12", "region": "World Report", "kicker": "World · Money", "title": "Who Gets Paid", "dek": "Hulk Hogan makes more in a month than most of the business makes in a year. But the biggest single paydays of July went to two men in Memphis, and the richest gates outside the WWF are in Mexico City.", "body": [["p", "With every major office in the world now putting talent on a formal pay scale, we finally have a picture of what wrestlers are making in the summer of 1991. It's a strange picture. Pay works one way in Stamford and Atlanta, another in Memphis and Mexico City, and a third in Tokyo, and the only thing every office agrees on is that the headliners get the biggest checks."], ["h", "The top of the mountain"], ["p", "Nobody is close to Hulk Hogan. The WWF Champion is on a $125,000-a-month salary, and that's before his cut of the WrestleFest main event at Busch Stadium on Sunday. Through the first nine days of July he's already earned about $36,000. The Ultimate Warrior is next at $90,000 a month. Below them is a flat tier at $40,000: Sid Justice, the Undertaker, Sgt. Slaughter and the newly signed Roddy Piper. Randy Savage, retired from the ring and sitting in the Superstars booth, still draws $35,000."], ["p", "The WWF can afford it. Six house shows to open the month brought in $1.31 million, and the full talent payroll, $1,056,500 a month including the managers and announcers, comes to roughly 23 percent of a normal month's gate. That's the lowest share of any major promotion in the world. Bobby Heenan ($15,000) and Mean Gene Okerlund ($12,000) out-earn most of the midcard."], ["h", "Atlanta after Flair"], ["p", "WCW's numbers tell you what losing Ric Flair did to the company. The top salaries are Lex Luger and Sting at $25,000 a month, a fifth of Hogan's. Barry Windham is at $16,000 and Rick Rude at $14,000 for a part-time schedule split with All Japan. The payroll is $342,500 a month against roughly $1 million in monthly gates, about 34 percent, and the office is counting on Turner money to cover the rest. Jim Ross is the highest-paid announcer at $8,000."], ["h", "The best payday in July was in Memphis"], ["p", "The USWA doesn't do salaries. Everybody is paid by the appearance, weekly, and the base rate doubles at the Mid South Coliseum and triples at a supershow. The main event also splits 12 percent of a supershow's gate. That's how Kerry Von Erich and Bret Hart walked out of Star Wars on July 1 with about $8,200 and $8,400 for one night's work. Each of them made more from that one match than Luger has made from WCW all month."], ["p", "Ric Flair is the top base rate in the territory at $625 an appearance, which means $1,875 on a supershow before any main-event split. If Flair headlines Reunion Arena in front of the 16,000 the office is projecting, he'll have the biggest single night in the country outside of a WWF pay-per-view. The catch is TV. Worldwide in Jonesboro drew $3,000 on July 3 and paid out $2,150 in talent. The TV shows lose money. The Coliseum and the supershows have to pay for them."], ["h", "Tokyo pays by the tour"], ["p", "In Japan, the money comes in one envelope at the end of the tour. Jumbo Tsuruta and Stan Hansen top All Japan at $30,000 for the 14-night Summer Action Series, with Misawa, Steve Williams and Terry Gordy at $22,000. Riki Choshu leads New Japan at $28,000 for Summer Crush, with Fujinami at $22,000 and Vader at $20,000."], ["p", "The foreigners who shuttle between promotions are paid only for the nights they work, and there are a lot of them. Hansen and Dan Spivey are also working Memphis. Rude and Abdullah are working Atlanta. Vader, Liger, Pillman and Sting are splitting time with WCW, and Bam Bam Bigelow is due back in Philadelphia. That matters most for New Japan, whose full-tour budget of $391,000 would be over 40 percent of the expected gate if everybody worked every night. They won't, and the office is counting on that."], ["h", "Arena Mexico is printing money"], ["p", "The surprise is CMLL. The July 5 Viernes Espectacular drew 14,000 and $189,000, and the 7/12 Friday did $135,000. Konnan tops the scale at $900 a night, doubled on Fridays, plus a share of 8 percent of the gate when he's in the main event. He's made $11,520 from five dates through July 12, more than anybody in WCW. Even with 23 to 26 workers on a Friday card, CMLL is paying out only about 24 percent of its gates."], ["h", "The small offices"], ["p", "In Britain, All Star Wrestling sells every holiday camp show to the resort for a flat $5,000, and the talent splits about $2,000 of it. Giant Haystacks and Big Daddy top the scale at $350 a night. Big Daddy hasn't worked a show yet. Bob Backlund, the special attraction, is at $300 and has had the best match on both cards."], ["p", "In Philadelphia, Bigelow is the Tri-State headliner at $600 a show, and booker Austin Idol pays himself $400. Super Summer Sizzler paid out $5,161 against a $12,600 gate. In Nashville, Power Pro champion Chris Jericho makes $60 a card. Bill Watts makes $100 to consult. Power Pro's Nashville crowds bring in about $1,000 to $2,000 a card, so the USWA is paying to grow its next generation."], ["h", "The bottom line"], ["p", "Hogan is the best-paid wrestler in the world by a mile. But if you're a top worker who isn't in the WWF, the place to be this summer is a promotion that pays on the gate: the Mid South Coliseum on a supershow night, or Arena Mexico on a Friday. A salary in Atlanta doesn't come close."]], "facts": [["Top salary", "Hulk Hogan, $125,000 a month"], ["Best one-night payday", "Bret Hart, ~$8,400 (Star Wars)"], ["Top tour fee", "Jumbo Tsuruta & Stan Hansen, $30,000"], ["Top per-night rate", "Konnan, $900 (×2 on Fridays)"], ["Lowest payroll share", "WWF, ~23%"], ["Power Pro champion", "Chris Jericho, $60 a card"]], "factTitle": "The Pay Sheet"}, "wwf-canadian-swing-wraps": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-12", "kicker": "WWF · Regina 7/11 and Winnipeg 7/12", "title": "The Canadian Swing Wraps", "dek": "Regina is the low point of July. Winnipeg is the best card of the tour. Sgt. Slaughter heads to WrestleFest with a win over the Warrior.", "factTitle": "The Two Nights", "facts": [["Regina", "4,200 · $63,000 · *1/4"], ["Winnipeg", "10,100 · $202,000 · **1/4"], ["Regina MOTN", "SATO d. Shane Douglas, ***"], ["Winnipeg MOTN", "High Energy d. Power & Glory, ***1/2"]], "body": [["h", "Regina: The Low Point"], ["p", "The Agridome drew 4,200 at $15 for $63,000, the smallest crowd and gate of July, and the card rated *1/4. Jimmy Snuka never made it; the story backstage is that he missed the drive from Saskatoon. Crush took his spot alongside Bushwhacker Luke, and the Beverly Brothers won when Coach tripped Luke."], ["p", "The main event went the wrong way for the Warrior. Sgt. Slaughter won by disqualification after Gen. Adnan interfered and then Road Warrior Hawk ran in on Slaughter. That gives Slaughter a win over the Warrior going into WrestleFest and opens an unplanned LOD vs. Slaughter thread."], ["p", "SATO and Shane Douglas had the best match (***). Big Boss Man looked good in seven minutes with Skinner (**3/4), and the Rockers beat Money Inc. when Michaels pinned IRS. Sid Justice lost to Jim Duggan by DQ when Harvey Wippleman interfered; the match rated -**."], ["h", "Winnipeg: The Best Card of the Tour"], ["p", "Winnipeg Arena drew 10,100 at $20 for $202,000, the third-biggest gate of July, and the card rated **1/4, the best of the tour. Every planned finish went as booked, and Snuka showed up and beat the Berzerker."], ["p", "Hulk Hogan and Big Boss Man beat Sgt. Slaughter and the Mountie when Boss Man pinned the Mountie with the Boss Man slam (**3/4), Hogan's best rating of July. Tito Santana and Jim Duggan beat Mr. Perfect and the Barbarian after Perfect walked out on his partner. High Energy and Power & Glory opened with the match of the night (***1/2); Owen Hart has now gone **3/4 or better in all four of his shows this week."], ["p", "Next: Milwaukee on Saturday, where nobody on the pay-per-view wrestles, and WrestleFest '91 at Busch Stadium on Sunday."]]}, "wwf-observer-regina-0711": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-11", "region": "Stamford Report", "kicker": "WWF · Agridome, July 11", "title": "Regina: Snuka No-Shows, Slaughter Walks Out with a Win", "dek": "The smallest crowd and gate of July, a -** Sid match, and a main event finish that went the wrong way three days before WrestleFest.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Venue", "Agridome"], ["City", "Regina, SK"], ["Date", "July 11, 1991"], ["Attendance", "4,200"], ["Average ticket", "$15.00"], ["Gate", "$63,000.00"], ["Card", "*1/4"]], "body": [["p", "The Canadian swing hit bottom in Regina. The WWF drew 4,200 paying $63,000 at a $15 average ticket, the smallest crowd and lowest gate of July, below even Des Moines, and the third straight drop since Calgary's $260,000. Cutting the ticket to $17 in Saskatoon and $15 here didn't help. The card rated *1/4, the weakest of the four nights in Alberta and Saskatchewan."], ["p", "It started with a no-show. Jimmy Snuka was booked with Bushwhacker Luke in the opener and never made the building. The dressing room story is that he missed the drive down Highway 11 from Saskatoon: either he slept through his wake-up call, or he got a late start and turned around after car trouble outside Davidson. The office wasn't happy, and he's booked in Winnipeg and on WrestleFest. Crush filled in, and the Beverlys beat Luke and Crush at 10:12 (**1/2) when Beau pinned Luke after the Coach got involved, which is better than anyone expected from that team."], ["p", "SATO beat Shane Douglas with a belly-to-back superplex at 10:43 for ***, the match of the night and their second *** match in four nights. After SATO's 42-second DQ loss in Saskatoon, it was the right call. Big Boss Man beat Skinner at 7:03 for **3/4. Boss Man's long matches with Dino Bravo have gone ** and 1/4*, so he may simply be better short."], ["p", "The Rockers beat Money Inc. at 19:14 (**3/4) when Michaels pinned IRS with a side suplex. They get the Nasty Boys for the tag titles Sunday, and Marty Jannetty's contract is coming due. If the belts are going on the Rockers in St. Louis, the office will want Jannetty signed first."], ["p", "Tenryu pinned Haku with a powerbomb at 12:09 but it rated only *3/4, the first SWS match on the loop that didn't land. Dino Bravo beat Koko B. Ware with the side suplex at 8:14 for *."], ["p", "The second half was rough. Jim Duggan beat Sid Justice by DQ at 10:57 when Harvey Wippleman interfered, and it rated -**. Sid has now been in two of the worst matches of the month (-***1/4 with Beefcake in Edmonton), and the ** with Hercules is the only time he's cleared a star on the loop. He's about to be pushed as Hogan's next challenger and needs short matches with strong opponents until then."], ["p", "The main was supposed to be the Ultimate Warrior over Sgt. Slaughter by DQ, with Gen. Adnan interfering to keep Slaughter strong. Adnan did his part, then Road Warrior Hawk, who wasn't on the show, came out after Slaughter, and the DQ went the other way. Slaughter won at 14:08 (*3/4) and goes into his WWF Title shot Sunday with a win over the Warrior, more than the office planned to give him. There's now an LOD vs. Slaughter thread nobody planned, and given Slaughter's gimmick the fans will want it."], ["p", "Bottom line: *1/4, the low point of the swing. SATO and Douglas, Boss Man and Skinner, and the Rockers held up the undercard, but the Snuka no-show, the -** Sid match and a main event that went the wrong way made for a rough night."]], "link": "WWF_REGI"}, "wwf-observer-winnipeg-0712": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-12", "region": "Stamford Report", "kicker": "WWF · Winnipeg Arena, July 12", "title": "Winnipeg Saves the Swing", "dek": "Best card of the month, a $202,000 gate, Owen Hart's fourth straight good match, and Mr. Perfect walks out on the Barbarian.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Venue", "Winnipeg Arena"], ["City", "Winnipeg, MB"], ["Date", "July 12, 1991"], ["Attendance", "10,100"], ["Average ticket", "$20.00"], ["Gate", "$202,000.00"], ["Card", "**1/4"]], "body": [["p", "Winnipeg saved the Canadian swing. The WWF drew 10,100 paying $202,000 at a $20 average ticket, the third-biggest gate of July behind MSG and Calgary, edging Edmonton by $1,000. The ticket went back up to $20 after Regina's $63,000 and the crowd still came, which suggests the problem in Saskatchewan was the markets, not the price. The card rated **1/4, the best of the tour, and every planned finish went as booked. July now stands at $1,575,280 with Milwaukee and the pay-per-view to come."], ["p", "The opener was the match of the night: High Energy over Power & Glory at 16:29 (***1/2) when Owen Hart pinned Paul Roma with the Rocket Launcher. Owen has gone **3/4 or better in all four of his shows this week: ***1/2 with Martel in Calgary, **3/4 with Perfect in Edmonton, *** with Koko in Saskatoon, and now this. He's set for the WrestleFest opener against the Beverlys, and there's a decent argument he's being wasted there."], ["p", "Tito Santana and Jim Duggan beat Mr. Perfect and the Barbarian at 14:22 (***) when Tito pinned the Barbarian with the flying forearm after Bobby Heenan got involved against Duggan. The twist: when the Barbarian reached for the tag, Perfect hopped off the apron and walked up the aisle with Heenan, who shouted all the way to the back that Perfect had a title to defend Sunday and wasn't getting hurt carrying the Barbarian. It got a big reaction, and the Barbarian didn't look happy getting off the mat. It was Duggan's best match of the loop."], ["p", "Hulk Hogan and Big Boss Man beat Sgt. Slaughter and the Mountie at 16:33 (**3/4) when Boss Man pinned the Mountie with the Boss Man slam, a little payback for the handcuffs in Calgary, despite Jimmy Hart's interference. That's Hogan's best rating of July by a mile, after 3/4* in Calgary and a DUD with the Warlord. Slaughter never took a fall, and after beating the Warrior in Regina he goes into St. Louis looking stronger than he has all month. Boss Man has now gone **3/4 on back-to-back nights."], ["p", "IRS made Virgil pass out in the Penalty at 12:06 (**1/4) with Hart's help, his first win over Virgil and a setup for Virgil vs. DiBiase on Sunday. The Undertaker beat Hercules with the Tombstone at 8:04 (**1/2); Hercules worked twice on the card and held up both times. Jake Roberts pinned Rick Martel with the DDT at 12:18 (**). Martel has lost to Owen, Tito and Jake in five days and been reliable in all three."], ["p", "Jimmy Snuka showed up after the Regina no-show and beat the Berzerker with the Superfly splash at 8:01 (3/4*) despite Mr. Fuji. Typhoon squashed Shane Douglas at 6:59 (*), which was the point, since he replaces Earthquake against Jake on Sunday. The low point was Koji Kitao over Crush with a big boot at 9:04 (-1/4*). Crush has now been in two of the worst matches of the month, and Kitao singles doesn't work; he's much better in a tag with Tenryu."], ["p", "Bottom line: **1/4 and $202,000, the best card of the tour and a strong finish to Canada. The two worries going into the weekend are the IC title match, where Greg Valentine's neck still needs a replacement challenger, and Sid Justice, who has to look like a monster on Sunday after a month of bad ratings."]], "link": "WWF_WINN"}, "wwf-diary-owen-0712": {"p": "wwf", "series": "diary", "by": "Owen Hart", "date": "1991-07-12", "place": "Winnipeg, the night before the flight to Milwaukee", "kicker": "From the Road · Winnipeg, July 12", "title": "Four Nights, Four Good Matches", "dek": "Owen Hart steals another show, watches Perfect walk out on the Barbarian, and wonders who gets Valentine's spot on Sunday.", "factTitle": "The Night", "facts": [["Show", "WWF house show, Winnipeg Arena"], ["Match", "High Energy d. Power & Glory"], ["Finish", "Rocket Launcher on Roma, 16:29"], ["Rating", "***1/2"]], "body": [["p", "Four nights, four good matches. I'm writing that down because I don't know when it'll happen again."], ["p", "Tonight Koko and I opened against Power & Glory, and from the first lockup the building was with us. Sixteen and a half minutes, and I don't think anybody went for a hot dog. Roma and Hercules are pros. They took everything we gave them and gave it right back. When I came off the top with the Rocket Launcher and landed on Roma, the noise was the loudest I've heard on this whole swing. Koko hugged me in the corner like we'd won the belts."], ["p", "It's been a strange week. Calgary on Monday was home, and I had Rick Martel, and it was the best match I've had in this company. Mom and Dad were in their seats. I could hear the old man over the whole Saddledome. Edmonton I lost to Mr. Perfect when Heenan got in the way, but Perfect is the best there is at making the other guy look like he belongs, and for eighteen minutes I did. Saskatoon was the tag with the Orient Express. Tonight was this. I keep waiting for a bad one."], ["p", "Then Regina happened to everybody else. I wasn't on that card, but I rode down with the crew and got an earful about Jimmy not making it. Tonight he was here, early, quiet, and he went out and beat the Berzerker. Nobody said a word to him about it. That's the business."], ["p", "Here's the part I'll only write in this book. Greg Valentine's neck is bad, and he was supposed to get Perfect for the Intercontinental Title on Sunday. Everybody in the dressing room knows somebody has to take that spot. I'm on the card in the opener against the Beverly Brothers, and I'll work that like it's the main event if that's where I stay. But I'd be lying if I said I didn't look at Perfect tonight when he walked out on the Barbarian and think about what I'd do with him for fifteen minutes in St. Louis."], ["p", "Nobody's told me anything. Milwaukee tomorrow, I'm not even on that one, and then Busch Stadium. I'll find out when I find out."], ["p", "Lights out. Koko's already asleep, and he snores like a chainsaw."]]}});

CONTRACTS.wwf = {"basis": "Monthly salary", "unit": "per month", "revenue": "July: $1,575,280 from the first eight house shows (about $197K a show). A full month of about 22 house shows plus a PPV runs roughly $4.5M at the gate.", "target": "Monthly talent payroll at about 30% of the month's gate (the WWF carries the biggest TV and production costs in the world).", "how": "Flat monthly salary for every contracted wrestler, paid whether or not he works every date. PPV main-event bonus: 5% of the PPV gate split by the main event. Injured wrestlers stay on salary. Managers and announcers are on the same scale. Vince McMahon owns the company and is not on the scale.", "classes": [], "rows": [["A", "Hulk Hogan", 125000, "WWF Champion"], ["A", "The Ultimate Warrior", 90000, ""], ["A", "Roddy Piper", 40000, "Signed 11/6 (35 days)"], ["A", "Sid Justice", 40000, ""], ["A", "The Undertaker", 40000, ""], ["A", "Sgt. Slaughter", 40000, ""], ["A", "Randy Savage", 35000, "Retired from the ring; commentary"], ["B", "Mr. Perfect", 30000, "Intercontinental Champion"], ["B", "Ted DiBiase", 30000, "Million Dollar Champion"], ["B", "Jake Roberts", 28000, ""], ["B", "Genichiro Tenryu", 25000, "SWS"], ["B", "Road Warrior Hawk", 22000, "Legion of Doom"], ["B", "Road Warrior Animal", 22000, "Legion of Doom"], ["B", "Big Boss Man", 20000, ""], ["B", "Earthquake", 20000, "Injured"], ["B", "Jim Duggan", 20000, ""], ["B", "Davey Boy Smith", 18000, "Back from AJPW ~7/29"], ["B", "Ricky Steamboat", 18000, "Injured, torn ACL"], ["B", "Brian Knobbs", 16000, "Tag Champion"], ["B", "Jerry Sags", 16000, "Tag Champion"], ["B", "Shawn Michaels", 15000, "Rockers"], ["B", "Marty Jannetty", 15000, "Rockers"], ["B", "Greg Valentine", 15000, "Injured"], ["C", "Irwin R. Schyster", 12000, ""], ["C", "Koji Kitao", 12000, "SWS"], ["C", "Dino Bravo", 12000, ""], ["C", "The Mountie", 10000, ""], ["C", "Brutus Beefcake", 10000, ""], ["C", "Tito Santana", 10000, ""], ["C", "Rick Martel", 10000, ""], ["C", "Typhoon", 10000, ""], ["C", "Jimmy Snuka", 8000, ""], ["C", "Haku", 8000, ""], ["C", "The Barbarian", 8000, ""], ["C", "Warlord", 8000, ""], ["C", "The Berzerker", 8000, ""], ["C", "Virgil", 8000, ""], ["C", "Owen Hart", 8000, ""], ["C", "Ax", 7000, "Demolition"], ["C", "Smash", 7000, "Demolition"], ["C", "Crush", 7000, "Demolition"], ["D", "Bushwhacker Luke", 6000, ""], ["D", "Bushwhacker Butch", 6000, "Injured"], ["D", "Hercules", 6000, "Power & Glory"], ["D", "Paul Roma", 6000, "Power & Glory"], ["D", "Kato", 5000, "Orient Express"], ["D", "Tanaka", 5000, "Orient Express"], ["D", "Beau Beverly", 5000, ""], ["D", "Blake Beverly", 5000, ""], ["D", "Koko B. Ware", 4000, ""], ["D", "Skinner", 4000, ""], ["D", "Shane Douglas", 4000, ""], ["D", "SATO", 4000, "SWS"], ["E", "Brooklyn Brawler", 2500, "Signed 11/6 (25 days)"], ["M", "Bobby Heenan", 15000, "Manager and commentary"], ["M", "Jimmy Hart", 10000, ""], ["M", "Miss Elizabeth", 8000, ""], ["M", "Paul Bearer", 6000, ""], ["M", "Mr. Fuji", 6000, ""], ["M", "Harvey Wippleman", 4000, ""], ["M", "Gen. Adnan", 4000, ""], ["M", "Coach", 4000, ""], ["O", "Mean Gene Okerlund", 12000, "Interviews"], ["O", "Gorilla Monsoon", 10000, "Challenge and Prime Time booth"], ["O", "Sean Mooney", 4000, "Event Center, WWF Update desk"], ["O", "Lord Alfred Hayes", 4000, "Interviews and commentary"], ["O", "Howard Finkel", 4000, "Ring announcer"]], "total": 1056500, "check": "Salaries total $1,056,500 a month: about 23% of a $4.5M month, leaving room for PPV bonuses."};

